=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** Router4's WAN interfaces — `eth4` (192.168.15.2/24, uplink to router5 via `link_r4_r5`) and `eth5` (172.25.1.1/24, uplink to router3 via `link_r4_r3`) — are both administratively/physically DOWN.

**Mechanism:** The lab runs RIPv2 for inter-zone routing. RIP activates only on interfaces whose addresses fall within the configured `network` statements. With eth4 and eth5 DOWN, router4 cannot send or receive RIP updates. Its routing table contains only one directly-connected route (`192.168.200.0/24`). When pc11–14 try to reach Location A (172.16.10.x / 172.16.20.x), the packet hits router4 (gateway 192.168.200.1), which has no route and returns ICMP Destination Net Unreachable. Simultaneously, the rest of the network never receives a RIP advertisement for 192.168.200.0/24 from router4, so no reverse path to Location C exists either.

**Localization:** Router4 — `eth4` state DOWN (confirmed via `ip addr show`); `eth5` state DOWN; router3's eth5 confirmed UP/LOWER_UP with ARP for router4's 172.25.1.1 returning "Host Unreachable" (no ARP reply from the down interface).

**Root cause:** Both WAN uplinks on router4 are in the DOWN state, completely isolating Location C from the RIP routing domain. Router3 is fully operational — the team's diagnosis is incorrect.

**Fix:** Bring router4's two WAN interfaces back up (`ip link set eth4 up && ip link set eth5 up` on router4). RIPv2 will reconverge within one to two update intervals (≤60 seconds), restoring full routing to and from Location C. No replacement of router3 is necessary.
=== END ===